  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through early next week. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThis evening cold air advection from the exiting cold front \nwill rapidly increase. With gradually clearing skies and NW\nflow, any rain showers leftover will likely switch to snow \nshowers. High pressure returns through the weekend, keeping \nthe pattern dry. Models are slowing a low pressure system to our\nwest, now show the cold front to cross the region sometime late\nweek. Upper air model soundings indicate a very dry boundary\nlayer, which any precip will need to fight against to make it to\nthe ground. Overall, no improvement to drought conditions are \nexpected with these showers. \n\nDeep layered ridging builds in Sunday through at least Wednesday\nand possibly into Thursday. There is some question if a \nnorthern stream shortwave passing over the top of the ridge will\nbring some rain to the Crown of Maine from Monday night into\nTuesday night as it passes, along with a weak surface warm\nfront. Even if there is rain, it should be less than 1/4\" with\nno impact to the drought. Especially noting that stream flows\nand groundwater levels remain below to well below normal across\na large portion of the region.\n\nThe main impact of the ridging building in will be a gradual\nmoderation of temperatures with continued dry weather. Highs\nincrease from the low to mid 50s on Sunday to mid 50s to mid 60s\non Monday. Depending on the extent of cloud cover/moisture\nassociated with the passing northern stream shortwave Monday \nnight/Tuesday. Tuesday could end up cooler than Monday, but \ntemperatures then increase into Thursday to levels close to \nthose forecast for Monday.\n\nThere is still quite a bit of uncertainty as to how fast the\ndeep layered ridge exits to the east late this week. This will\nultimately determine when the next storm system impacts the\nregion. Given the highly amplified nature of the flow, the\nslower solutions make more sense overall. So it would not be\nsurprising if the chances of rain on Friday end up getting\npushed back into the weekend. Given this uncertainty, it is to\nsoon to specify what if any impact this system could have on the\nregion.\n\nFortunately winds are expected to remain relatively light and\nminimum relative humidities generally above 45% through this \ntime frame, so there are minimal if any fire weather concerns \nthrough the end of the work week.",
